Because the box contents are fixed, your wife does **not** need modifiers or a second data-entry screen. She sells one of six variations in Square, and the application identifies the variation by its immutable Square catalogue ID:

| Square variation sold                  | Application inventory effect                         |
| -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| Christmas Tree — Individual            | None; Square deducts 1 automatically                 |
| Christmas Tree — Box of 7              | Deduct 7 Christmas Tree candles + 1 seven-candle box |
| Bulk Christmas Tree Small — Individual | None; Square deducts 1 automatically                 |
| Bulk Christmas Tree Small — Box of 3   | Deduct 3 small candles + 1 applicable box            |
| Bulk Christmas Tree Tall — Individual  | None; Square deducts 1 automatically                 |
| Bulk Christmas Tree Tall — Box of 3    | Deduct 3 tall candles + 1 applicable box             |

Square makes POS-created orders available through the Orders API, and `SearchOrders` can retrieve completed orders incrementally. Square inventory is tracked at catalogue-variation level, but its general Inventory API does not model arbitrary bundles and their separate packaging components, so the application can supply that recipe logic and post adjustments with `BatchChangeInventory`. ([Square][1])

Square now also has a Retail Plus stock-conversion feature for selling the same product in different units. However, it requires Square for Retail Plus and still does not represent the separate cardboard/package box consumed by your bundle. The prompt below deliberately uses one consistent application-managed recipe model and avoids mixing the two approaches. ([Square][2])

